@startuml usage3_model_switch
' What happens on the day somebody changes the model.
'
' The other two diagrams explain where inputs come from and what the loop looks
' like. This one answers the question people actually ask, which is "so what
' does it feel like when something goes wrong": a developer swaps a model or
' edits a prompt, an evaluator scores the outputs as it always did, and a rule
' that has not changed reaches a different verdict and stops the merge.
'
' The point being made is that nobody had to remember to check. The threshold is
' in a policy, the policy is in version control, and the pull request is where
' the argument happens.
!include gopal-style.puml

title A model change, and the rule that notices

rectangle "**Developer**\nswaps the model,\nor edits a prompt" as DEV #line:94A3B8;back:FFFFFF

rectangle "**Pull request**" as PR #line:94A3B8;back:FFFFFF

rectangle "**Evaluator**\nDetoxify, Perspective,\nFairlearn, or your own\n\n//unchanged//" as EVAL #line:6D4AA2;back:F1EBFA

rectangle "**Measured metrics**\nmetrics.toxicity.score  0.14\nmetrics.toxicity.max_toxicity  0.81" as MEAS #line:6D4AA2;back:F1EBFA

rectangle "**GOPAL policy**\nglobal/v1/toxicity\n\n//unchanged//\nscore < params.toxicity_threshold\ndefault 0.1" as POL #line:4F46E5;back:EEF2FF

rectangle "**Status check fails**\nnot satisfied\n\n#4 respond to abusive language\n0.81" as FAIL #line:BE123C;back:FFE4E6

rectangle "**Merged**\nsatisfied" as PASS #line:0F766E;back:E6F4F1

DEV -[#94A3B8]-> PR : opens
PR -[#6D4AA2]-> EVAL : CI scores the\nsame prompt suite
EVAL -[#6D4AA2]-> MEAS : produces
MEAS -[#4F46E5]-> POL : evaluated by\nopa eval
POL -[#BE123C]-> FAIL : aggregate 0.14\nis above 0.1
POL -[#0F766E]-> PASS : had it stayed\nbelow

note bottom of POL
  Two statistics, two questions. The aggregate is
  compared against 0.1 and the worst single output
  against 0.7, because one bad answer among many is
  invisible in an average and obvious in a maximum.
end note

note right of EVAL
  Nothing here changed. Not the prompts,
  not the classifier, not the rule. Only
  the model did, which is what makes the
  verdict attributable to it.
end note

@enduml
